{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774301671,"nonce":"9+zKAKu82srpbcER4Auaks5w1xI=","title":"Review sessions should not get suspended:too-complex","message":"When a review session (review-*) hits MAX_ORPHAN_RESUMES, session_monitor.maybe_resume_orphan() sets it to suspended:too-complex with a message telling the user to \"split into smaller sub-tasks\". This is wrong for review sessions:\n\n1. A review crashing 3 times doesn't mean the task is too complex — it means the review itself failed (context limit, auth issue, etc.)\n2. The coder session gets stuck in `reviewing` status with no way forward\n3. The \"split into sub-tasks\" message is nonsensical for a review\n\nFix: In maybe_resume_orphan(), when a review session hits the orphan limit:\n- Set the review session to suspended:review-failed (or just clean it up)\n- Transition the coder session from `reviewing` to `waiting:human-review` so the human can review directly\n- Post a different notification: \"Auto-review failed after N attempts — falling back to human review\"\n\nThis should NOT change the too-complex behavior for coder sessions.","files":null}]}